Taking the Lojban alone, if {pimu loi djacu} means half the glass of
water (though it doesn't, does it -- it means half of all water), then
it would be what you get if you take pa loi djacu and chop it in
half. And re loi djacu would be what you get if you take pa loi
djacu and duplicate it. In that case, {piro} would mean the same
as {pa}, which is not unreasonable, since n/n = 1. So Q lo(i) 
would mean "Q * a single". That is perfectly coherent to me (today).

But you say it works like English. I don't actually know how this
bit of English works; I've not studied it except cursorily. Although
I do in fact think English is more logical than people give it
credit for, by Ideal Loglan/Lojban standards of logicality it is
not so logical, and the claim I usually mean to make is in fact that
when people speak English they are using English to express logical
meanings -- that is, logic infuses all our thoughts and some of
that logic filters into English itself, though with an as-yet
undiscovered degree of ambiguity and kludginess.

